Problem:
You need to limit the number of simultaneous connections based on a predefined key, typically the client’s IP address, to prevent abuse or excessive load.

Solution:
Create a shared memory zone to store connection metrics.
Use the limit_conn directive to restrict the number of open connections per key.


http {
    # Create shared memory zone for tracking connections per client IP
    limit_conn_zone $binary_remote_addr zone=limitbyaddr:10m;

    # Set HTTP status code when connection limit is exceeded
    limit_conn_status 429;

    server {
        listen 80;
        server_name example.com;

        # Limit to 40 connections per client IP
        limit_conn limitbyaddr 40;

        location / {
            proxy_pass http://backend;
        }
    }
}


Explanation:
limit_conn_zone $binary_remote_addr zone=limitbyaddr:10m;
Defines a shared memory zone named limitbyaddr.
$binary_remote_addr is the predefined key (client IP in binary form).
10m is the size of shared memory (10 MB).
limit_conn limitbyaddr 40;
Limits connections per key (here, per IP) to 40 simultaneous connections.
limit_conn_status 429;
Returns HTTP 429 Too Many Requests when the limit is exceeded.
Default is 503, but 429 is preferable for client-side errors.
Both limit_conn and limit_conn_status can be used in http, server, or location context.
Important Considerations
Using IP as the key can be risky if multiple users share the same IP (e.g., behind NAT).
You can use other variables, such as session cookies, for more precise limits at the application level.
The limit_conn_zone directive is only valid in the http context.
Testing Connection Limits
Testing live traffic is often tricky. You can simulate and analyze without blocking users:
Use dry-run mode for request limiting (similar to limit_req_dry_run) to log status without enforcing limits.
Check $limit_req_status in access logs to evaluate traffic behavior.

Possible values of $limit_req_status:
PASSED → Request allowe
DELAYED → Request delayed
REJECTED → Request rejected
DELAYED_DRY_RUN → Delayed in dry-run mode
REJECTED_DRY_RUN → Rejected in dry-run mode
This allows you to fine-tune limits safely before enforcing them.

